{title: I Heard the Bells on Christmas Day}
{artist: Henry Wadsworth Longfellow & John Calkin}
{key: Eb}
{time: 4/4}
{tempo: 84}

Verse 1
I [Eb]heard the bells on [Ab]Christmas [Eb]day their [C]old fa[F]miliar [Bb]carols play,
and [Eb]wild and [Cm]sweet the [Ab]words re[Eb]peat of [Ab]peace on [Eb]earth, good [Bb]will to [Eb]men.

Verse 2
And [Eb]thought how, as the [Ab]day [Eb]had come, [C]The belfries [Bb]of all
Christendom [Eb]Had rolled [Cm]along th'unbroken [Ab]song Of [Ab]peace on [Eb]earth, good [Bb]will to [Eb]men.

Verse 3
Till [Eb]ringing, singing on its [Ab]way [Eb]The world [C]revolved from [Bb]night to
day, [Eb]A voice, [Cm]a chime, [Ab]a chant sublime Of [Ab]peace on [Eb]earth, good [Bb]will to [Eb]men.

Verse 4
And [Eb]in despair I bowed my [Ab]head [Eb]"There is [C]no peace on [Bb]earth," I
said, [Eb]"For hate [Cm]is strong and [Ab]mocks the song [Ab]Of peace [Eb]on earth, good [Bb]will to [Eb]men."

Verse 5
Then [Eb]pealed the bells more loud [Ab]and [Eb]deep: "God [C]is not dead, [Bb]nor doth
He [Eb]sleep; The [Cm]wrong shall fail, [Ab]the right prevail [Ab]With peace [Eb]on earth, good [Bb]will to [Eb]men."
